web: respect `extensions.webWorker` in a remote window, as the desktop does (`false`: never, `'auto'`: when a web extension needs it)

The setting `extensions.webWorker` ("Enable running extensions in a web worker": `true`, `false`, `'auto'`, the default) is registered
for every workbench, and the desktop's extension service decides with it whether and when to start the web worker extension host
(determineLocalWebWorkerExtHostEnablement): `false` never starts it, `'auto'` starts it lazily (ExtensionHostStartup.LazyAutoStart),
only when an extension that runs in it is installed and enabled, `true` at once. The web workbench always creates that host, at once.
In a remote window every extension that is installed on the server runs there anyway; the local web worker host is only needed for
browser-only extensions. An embedder whose page cannot host it (a Content-Security-Policy without frame-src for the host's iframe and
without blob: for its worker) gets a host that fails to start on every load, with a CSP violation and an "unknown error" in the log;
an embedder that can host it (in an isolated frame) still starts an iframe and a worker on every load that most windows never use.

This makes the web workbench honour the setting in a remote window exactly as the desktop does: with `false` no web worker extension
host is created; with `'auto'` it is created lazily, and then, as on the desktop, it takes its extensions from the extension registry
(its data provider's restart path), since it starts only once the registry is known. `true` and a window without a remote are unchanged
(there the web worker host is the only one).

